     The SSIIGGAADDDDSSEETT() macro adds _s_i_g_n_o to _s_e_t.  No effort is made to ensure
     that _s_i_g_n_o is a valid signal number.

     The SSIIGGDDEELLSSEETT() macro removes _s_i_g_n_o from _s_e_t.  No effort is made to en‐
     sure that _s_i_g_n_o is a valid signal number.

     The SSIIGGEEMMPPTTYYSSEETT() macro clears all signals in _s_e_t.

     The SSIIGGFFIILLLLSSEETT() macro sets all signals in _s_e_t.

     The SSIIGGIISSMMEEMMBBEERR() macro determines if _s_i_g_n_o is set in _s_e_t.

     The SSIIGGIISSEEMMPPTTYY() macro determines if _s_e_t does not have any signals set.

     The SSIIGGNNOOTTEEMMPPTTYY() macro determines if _s_e_t has any signals set.

     The SSIIGGSSEETTEEQQ() macro determines if two signal sets are equal; that is,
     the same signals are set in both.

     The SSIIGGSSEETTNNEEQQ() macro determines if two signal sets differ; that is, if
     any signal set in one is not set in the other.

     The SSIIGGSSEETTOORR() macro ORs the signals set in _s_e_t_2 into _s_e_t_1.

     The SSIIGGSSEETTAANNDD() macro ANDs the signals set in _s_e_t_2 into _s_e_t_1.

     The SSIIGGSSEETTNNAANNDD() macro NANDs the signals set in _s_e_t_2 into _s_e_t_1.

     The SSIIGG__CCAANNTTMMAASSKK() macro clears the SIGKILL and SIGSTOP signals from _s_e_t.
     These two signals cannot be blocked or caught and SSIIGG__CCAANNTTMMAASSKK() is used
     in code where signals are manipulated to ensure this policy is enforced.

     The SSIIGG__SSTTOOPPSSIIGGMMAASSKK() macro clears the SIGSTOP, SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN, and
     SIGTTOU signals from _s_e_t.  SSIIGG__SSTTOOPPSSIIGGMMAASSKK() is used to clear stop sig‐
     nals when a process is waiting for a child to exit or exec, and when a
     process is continuing after having been suspended.

     The SSIIGG__CCOONNTTSSIIGGMMAASSKK() macro clears the SIGCONT signal from _s_e_t.
     SSIIGG__CCOONNTTSSIIGGMMAASSKK() is called when a process is stopped.

     The SSIIGGPPEENNDDIINNGG() macro determines if the given process has any pending
     signals that are not masked.  If the process has a pending signal and the
     process is currently being traced, SSIIGGPPEENNDDIINNGG() will return true even if
     the signal is masked.

     The ccuurrssiigg() function returns the signal number that should be delivered
     to process _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c.  If there are no signals pending, zero is re‐
     turned.

     The eexxeeccssiiggss() function resets the signal set and signal stack of a
     process in preparation for an execve(2).  The process lock for _p must be
     held before eexxeeccssiiggss() is called.

     The iissssiiggnnaall() function determines if there are any pending signals for
     process _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c that should be caught, or cause this process to ter‐
     minate or interrupt its current system call.  If process _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c is
     currently being traced, ignored signals will be handled and the process
     is always stopped.  Stop signals are handled and cleared right away by
     iissssiiggnnaall() unless the process is a member of an orphaned process group
     and the stop signal originated from a TTY.  The process spin lock for
     _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c may be acquired and released.  The _s_i_g_a_c_t_s structure
     _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c_-_>_p___s_i_g_a_c_t_s must be locked before calling iissssiiggnnaall() and may
     be released and reacquired during the call.  The process lock for
     _t_d_-_>_t_d___p_r_o_c must be acquired before calling iissssiiggnnaall() and may be re‐
